this is where a 19 year old football fan  was killed in the northern greek city of  thessaloniki  the street is now lined with candles and  flowers  alkish campanos was stamped to death  outside the aristosaloniki club stadium  he's believed to have been killed by  supporters of a rival team after a brawl  the case has led to a national debate  about violence at sporting events  we can't get over it my entire family  has been affected i have a 17 year old  grandson who wears the team's yellow  jersey all day and now we feel afraid  i'm 72 years old and i've been going to  the stadium since i was eight we never  had these types of  situations at least nine suspects have  been arrested but many say it's not  enough  the outrage is fueling calls for tighter  rules and punishment  under pressure the government announced  it will submit a bill in the coming  weeks to parliament with stricter  penalties and more police controls  combating violence among football fans  is an obligation we have towards greek  society the youth and the millions of  fans in our country in the past two  years significant steps have been taken  the government will continue in this  direction and we expect everyone in  society to contribute to eliminate  sports violence  the sport has been plagued by violence  on and off the field in recent years  these photographs of makeshift weapons  are among the items confiscated by  police a sign that the threat of  violence is always lurking and now the  murder of alkies is pushing the issue to  the forefront  i feel awful about it how can you not i  wonder what these people are thinking  how can they live it all seems  unthinkable all this violence taking  place  yellow jerseys of the adi's football  club have been placed in the  neighborhood where alkies was murdered  people here say it's a reminder to  authorities and the community of a  senseless tragedy  katie lopez al jazeera
 

